## Technical Invention Summary

The invention relates to an adjustable-length connecting rod for an internal combustion engine, in particular a connecting rod for varying the compression ratio of a cylinder during engine operation. The connecting rod includes a length adjustment device whose operating state is controlled by an electrically switchable valve that is actuated using inductively generated electrical power.

The connecting rod comprises a small-end bearing for connection to a piston pin, a big-end bearing for connection to a crankshaft journal, and an adjustment mechanism arranged between the small-end and big-end regions. The adjustment mechanism may include one or more hydraulic chambers, pistons, eccentric elements, locking elements, or other displacement mechanisms capable of changing an effective length of the connecting rod between at least two positions. By changing the effective distance between the piston pin axis and the crankshaft journal axis, the compression ratio of the associated engine cylinder can be selectively altered.

A control valve is integrated into or mounted on the connecting rod and is configured to control the flow of hydraulic fluid, the release or locking of hydraulic pressure, or the actuation state of the length adjustment mechanism. The valve is electrically switchable and preferably includes an inductive actuator, such as a solenoid coil, electromagnetic plunger, magnetically movable valve member, or similar electromagnetic switching element. When energized, the valve changes state to enable or prevent adjustment of the connecting rod length.

Electrical energy for operating the valve is generated directly on or near the moving connecting rod by electromagnetic induction. One or more permanent magnets are mounted on a stationary or rotating engine component, such as the crankcase, crankshaft, crank web, counterweight, or another component positioned adjacent to the path of the connecting rod. A corresponding induction coil is arranged on the connecting rod, or alternatively the magnetic elements and coil may be arranged in complementary positions such that relative movement between the connecting rod and the magnets produces a changing magnetic flux through the coil. During engine operation, the reciprocating and rotational motion of the connecting rod relative to the magnets induces an electrical voltage in the coil.

The induced electrical energy is supplied to the electrically switchable valve either directly or through an electrical conditioning circuit. The conditioning circuit may include a rectifier, voltage limiter, capacitor, energy storage element, switching circuit, or control electronics. The system may store induced energy temporarily and release it at a desired crank angle, load condition, engine speed, or control command to actuate the valve. The valve may be switched by selectively allowing induced current to flow through the actuator coil or by using the induced power to drive a local electronic control unit mounted on the connecting rod.

In one embodiment, magnets are fixed to the crankcase so that the connecting rod-mounted induction coil passes through or near a magnetic field region during each crankshaft revolution. In another embodiment, magnets are arranged on the crankshaft or crank webs, producing a changing magnetic field at the connecting rod as the crankshaft rotates. The placement, polarity, and number of magnets may be selected to generate sufficient voltage pulses at predetermined crankshaft angular positions. These pulses may be used both as an energy source and as a timing reference for valve actuation.

The electrically switchable valve may be configured as a normally open, normally closed, bistable, or pulse-actuated valve. A bistable valve is particularly advantageous because electrical energy is required only to change the valve state, reducing the required induced power. The valve may control hydraulic oil supplied from the crankshaft bearing region, from a connecting rod oil channel, or from an internal reservoir. Depending on the valve position, hydraulic pressure may extend, retract, lock, or release the length adjustment device.

The invention eliminates or reduces the need for slip rings, wired electrical connections, batteries, or complex mechanical control systems on the rapidly moving connecting rod. By generating power inductively from magnets mounted on the crankcase or crankshaft, reliable valve actuation can be achieved in the harsh environment of an engine crankcase. The system enables precise and rapid switching of the connecting rod length adjustment mechanism while maintaining compact packaging and compatibility with conventional crankshaft and connecting rod architectures.

Key advantages include:

- electrically controlled adjustment of connecting rod length during engine operation;
- inductive energy generation without direct wired connections to the moving rod;
- use of crankcase-mounted or crankshaft-mounted magnets as the magnetic field source;
- reduced mechanical complexity compared with purely hydraulic or centrifugal switching systems;
- improved control of variable compression ratio operation;
- potential crank-angle-synchronized valve actuation using induced voltage pulses;
- robust integration within existing engine lubrication and crankcase environments.

The invention is applicable to variable compression ratio engines, efficiency-optimized combustion engines, hybrid powertrains, turbocharged engines requiring knock mitigation, and any reciprocating machine in which controlled variation of connecting rod effective length is desirable.
